{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310572,"nonce":"emq70ifWfeUjyc7wx+13QKD3S1Q=","title":"THREE defects share one root, ALL FIXED 2026-09-06 — every parent-chain walk in MIR reads the CURRENT module's HIR/type tables, so a parent ...","message":"**One repro exposes all three** (`$SP/ctorv/*`, four files):\n```\npk/Base.hx   class Base { public function new() {} public function hi() return \"base\"; }\nMid.hx       class Mid extends pk.Base { public function mid() return 7; }\nMain.hx      class Main extends Mid { static function main() { ... } }\n```\n\n1. **`new Mid()` from another class** — FIXED 07125a21. `inherited_constructor`\n   (ir/mir/helpers/alloc.rs) walks `current_hir_types`, so a parent from another\n   module ends the walk; the call fell through to a forward-ref stub keyed\n   `Mid.new`, which can NEVER bind because Mid declares no constructor. Fix:\n   `sig_index::constructor_owner` walks the recorded `extends` by NAME to the\n   ancestor that declares one, and the stub is keyed on THAT.\n2. **`super()` to a cross-module parent** — FIXED 77ad2eb3. Reported E0100\n   \"Parent constructor not found\" and failed the whole file. The parent's TypeId\n   is often not in this context's type table at all (not Class, not placeholder,\n   not alias), so `receiver_type_class_name` returns None -- the CURRENT class's\n   recorded `extends` is what names the parent (`super_constructor_fqn_key`).\n3. **An inherited method's return type** — FIXED e12360df in the TYPER, as\n   predicted. `resolve_method_symbol` (tast/ast_lowering/expr/calls.rs) asks\n   `resolve_class_method_symbol` for the receiver's OWN class, and all of its\n   strategies (local table, exact `Class.method` qname, package-suffix) are keyed\n   on that one class; an ancestor's method matches none, so the call got a\n   PLACEHOLDER symbol with no type. Fix: walk `class_parents` at that call site.\n   Two things that did NOT work and were reverted: an\n   `external_function_return_types` table plumbed to the MIR call site, and the\n   same ancestor walk in `fields.rs` `resolve_in_class` (field access is a\n   different path; both were inert). Original symptom below. `m.mid()` on a subclass whose\n   parent is elsewhere returns correctly (the callee declares `-\u003e i32` and the\n   call is direct, NOT a stub), but the result register is `Ptr`, so\n   `convert_to_string` takes the `Ptr(_)` arm -\u003e `haxe_std_string_ptr` on the\n   integer 7 -\u003e the process EXITS 0 PRINTING NOTHING. A String return prints\n   `\u003cunknown type N\u003e` instead.\n   **Not fixable in MIR**: resolved_method.rs already prefers the callee's\n   declared return when the function is in the local module, and adding an\n   `external_function_return_types` table plumbed from\n   `crossmodule.rs`/`driver.rs` changed NOTHING (attempt reverted, unexercised).\n   The Ptr comes from the TYPER: `m.mid()` is typed unresolved because tast\n   never resolves a member inherited from a class in another module. That is the\n   same root as the memory line \"29 blocked on cross-module inherited calls\".\n\n**Watch for**: a silent `exit 0` with no output is this bug's signature -- worse\nthan a trap, because nothing is printed. See\n[[bugs_xmodule_resolution_disease_cluster.md]],\n[[bugs_conformance_scores_the_tiered_path_not_llvm.md]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_cross_module_inheritance_family` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}